								<div class="title" id="post-title-4438315">
																		Site Criticism Team - Staff Review
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/themightymcb" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(4483018); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=4483018&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608349838" alt="TheMightyMcB" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=4483018)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/themightymcb" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(4483018); return false;" >TheMightyMcB</a></span> <span class="odate time_1575483559 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">04 Dec 2019 18:19</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4438315">
									

<p>I feel bad voting the way I did for this, so let me briefly explain why.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I've upvoted vent pieces before. Honestly, they're some of my favorites. Tales like <a href="/crying-over-coffee">Crying Over Coffee</a> and <a href="http://www.scp-wiki.net/i-didnt-want-to-see-you-die-like-this/comments/show">i didn't want to see you die like this</a> are probably some of the most powerful pieces I've read on site. This, however, didn't carry the same weight.</p>
<p>I think that's because of your framing. The previous two works were told from the perspective of original characters made by the authors, with those characters going through experiences that were extremely painful and personal to those authors. In addition, those pieces also had fantastic prose and gut-wrenching emotion.</p>
<p>In comparison, this piece is lacking those attributes that make the other pieces so strong. The character isn't one of your own design, so the entire read, I found myself comparing your Jack Bright to the Jack Bright of other tales. They're hardly even the same character to be honest, which distracted me from your message. Furthermore, rather than using the situation that your characters are found in to tell the emotional story, you chose to have Bright monologue at the reader, which gave away your pathos too early in the game. Rather than setting a tone and letting the reader figure out what was happening, you essentially told us &quot;Jack Bright thinks dying sucks and you shouldn't do it if you can help it&quot;.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>I appreciate the time you took to pour your heart out like this, and I encourage you to find somebody to talk to, but this tale just didn't do it for me. Sorry.</p>
